# count: 9
#
# One line per finding site the fixture proves. The self-test requires every
# substring below to appear in the fixture's output, so a check that stops
# examining anything goes missing here and is named rather than passing
# silently. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# `# count:` above is the number of entries and is checked. An empty file was
# already caught; a file one line shorter was not, and deleting a single line
# is the cheapest way to disarm one check without anything noticing. Adding an
# entry means bumping the count, which is a deliberate edit rather than a
# silent one.
#
# This exists because an exit code proved only that *some* check fired: four
# checks were deleted outright and the runner's output was identical.
#
# Each line is matched with grep -F, so it is a literal substring and not a
# pattern. Keep them short and distinctive — long enough to name one check,
# short enough to survive a reworded message.

